WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:09.680
In President Trump's impeachment trial,
senators swore to do impartial justice.

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:13.460
How can they keep such an oath? How can
partisan senators be impartial?

00:00:13.460 --> 00:00:18.060
They have to understand two things: what's on
trial and what the Senate was created to be.

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:23.509
First, what is on trial? The president,
but also the impeachment itself.

00:00:23.509 --> 00:00:27.439
The Constitution empowers the Senate to try
impeachments, so the Senate must consider

00:00:27.439 --> 00:00:32.480
not just factual questions, but legal and
Prudential questions too. Second, what was

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:37.220
the Senate created to be? A counterweight
to both the House and the president.

00:00:37.220 --> 00:00:42.000
The founders expected the Senate to embody a
mix of accountability and statesmanship.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:45.559
It's meant to temper the passions of the
House in passing laws and to play

00:00:45.559 --> 00:00:49.160
a significant role in the president's
administration of domestic laws and

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:53.210
foreign policy. An impeachment trial is
the Senate's ultimate opportunity to

00:00:53.210 --> 00:00:56.420
serve both those roles when they are needed most.

00:00:56.420 --> 00:01:00.340
That is the impartial justice that
impeachment calls upon them to do.

00:01:00.340 --> 00:01:03.720
Impeachment does not need the Senators
to transform into jurors.

00:01:03.720 --> 00:01:07.560
It needs senators to become, truly, the Senate.

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:10.900
In impeachment, can the Senate
and the House be truly impartial?

